From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 17:24:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: apple: power ports off for a sleep that
 powers the SoC down

A sleep that powers the SoC down (mem with apple-s2r) takes ACIO's M3
coprocessor, the NHI's rings and the routers' links with it. Nothing in
the driver knows: the ports stay powered through the notifier chain, the
NHI's suspend keeps the routers awake (QUIRK_NO_SYSTEM_SLEEP, and
QUIRK_KEEP_TUNNELS for a kept PCIe link), and at the resume the
connection manager talks to an NHI and a coprocessor that are gone,
behind devices that are still bound.

The driver already has the way through such a sleep, for
suspend-to-idle behind s2idle_teardown: at PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE, before
tasks freeze, a connected port is powered off (apple_cio_stop()), and
after the sleep the port controller restores it from a fresh cable read
(sleep_restart_owed, typec_thunderbolt_switch_notify_restore()). Take
that way for every sleep that isn't suspend-to-idle, whatever
s2idle_teardown says. The devices behind the port's tunnels are then
removed before the sleep and found again after it, as with the
parameter.

A port with a PCIe tunnel is skipped by that teardown, as its endpoints
may unregister PM notifiers inside the chain and its storage must not
go away under running programs. A deeper sleep would take it down all
the same, so such a port now refuses that sleep, with the reason in the
log, and keeps suspend-to-idle as before.

The choice is a pure helper (apple-cio-sleep.h) with a KUnit suite,
apple-cio-sleep, next to the DP-IN tests.

TO-VERIFY on the J604 (deep sleep there is still refused elsewhere: a
bound dcpext and the GPU), dev-loop checks on a kboot-s2r boot after
`echo mem > /sys/power/state`:
 - a USB4 or Thunderbolt dock with a keyboard or a USB stick: "powering
   off port for deep sleep" before, "restarting port after system
   sleep" after, and the dock and its devices back (boltctl list,
   lsusb -t);
 - with an NVMe enclosure (PCIe tunnel) attached and mounted: the sleep
   is refused with "refusing deep sleep: a PCIe tunnel is up", and the
   disk keeps working;
 - suspend-to-idle with s2idle_teardown=0 is unchanged.
Keeping a PCIe tunnel's devices across such a sleep (macOS keeps them)
needs the PCIe-C link and the endpoints to come back after the power
loss; that is not implemented here.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 MAINTAINERS                           |  1 +
 drivers/thunderbolt/apple-cio-sleep.h | 43 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/thunderbolt/apple-dpin-test.c | 45 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c           | 46 +++++++++++++++++++--------
 4 files changed, 121 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 drivers/thunderbolt/apple-cio-sleep.h

diff --git a/MAINTAINERS b/MAINTAINERS
index 18d06932e0..5a7f14406c 100644
--- a/MAINTAINERS
+++ b/MAINTAINERS
@@ -2803,6 +2803,7 @@ F:	drivers/spi/spi-apple.c
 F:	drivers/spmi/spmi-apple-controller.c
 F:	drivers/thermal/apple-pmp*
 F:	drivers/thunderbolt/.kunitconfig.apple-dpin
+F:	drivers/thunderbolt/apple-cio-sleep.h
 F:	drivers/thunderbolt/apple-dpin*
 F:	drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c
 F:	drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple-state-test.c
diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/apple-cio-sleep.h b/drivers/thunderbolt/apple-cio-sleep.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..bb94e42444
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/apple-cio-sleep.h
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only */
+/*
+ * Apple ACIO: what a connected port does at the start of a system sleep, kept in a
+ * header so that the choice can be tested without the hardware.
+ */
+#ifndef _APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_H
+#define _APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_H
+
+#include <linux/types.h>
+
+enum apple_cio_sleep_action {
+	APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_KEEP, /* The port stays up through the sleep */
+	APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_POWER_OFF, /* Powered off now, restored after the sleep */
+	APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_REFUSE, /* The sleep is refused */
+};
+
+/**
+ * apple_cio_sleep_action() - what a port does at PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE
+ * @deep: the sleep powers the SoC down (not suspend-to-idle)
+ * @teardown: the s2idle_teardown module parameter
+ * @connected: the port is up with a cable, and neither being removed nor disabled for good
+ * @pcie: a PCIe tunnel is requested or populated on the port
+ *
+ * A sleep that powers the SoC down takes ACIO's coprocessor, the NHI and the routers'
+ * links with it, behind devices that are still bound: the port is powered off first and
+ * restored after the sleep, as s2idle_teardown does for suspend-to-idle. A PCIe tunnel is
+ * not torn down from the notifier chain (its endpoints may unregister PM notifiers there)
+ * and its storage must not go away under running programs, so such a port keeps its
+ * suspend-to-idle and refuses a deeper sleep.
+ *
+ * Return: the action.
+ */
+static inline enum apple_cio_sleep_action
+apple_cio_sleep_action(bool deep, bool teardown, bool connected, bool pcie)
+{
+	if (!connected || (!deep && !teardown))
+		return APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_KEEP;
+	if (pcie)
+		return deep ? APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_REFUSE : APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_KEEP;
+	return APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_POWER_OFF;
+}
+
+#endif /* _APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_H */
diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/apple-dpin-test.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/apple-dpin-test.c
index 0d4891ad74..5f9b5316af 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/apple-dpin-test.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/apple-dpin-test.c
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
 #include <linux/errno.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 
+#include "apple-cio-sleep.h"
 #include "apple-dpin-platform.h"
 #include "apple-dpin-state.h"
 #include "apple-dpin-request.h"
@@ -1229,3 +1230,47 @@ static struct kunit_suite apple_dpin_suite = {
 };
 
 kunit_test_suite(apple_dpin_suite);
+
+/* What a port does at the start of a sleep, for each kind of sleep and port. */
+static void apple_cio_sleep_action_test(struct kunit *test)
+{
+	static const struct {
+		bool deep, teardown, connected, pcie;
+		enum apple_cio_sleep_action action;
+	} cases[] = {
+		/* Suspend-to-idle without s2idle_teardown: every port stays up */
+		{ false, false, true, false, APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_KEEP },
+		{ false, false, true, true, APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_KEEP },
+		/* With it: powered off, and a port with a PCIe tunnel stays up */
+		{ false, true, true, false, APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_POWER_OFF },
+		{ false, true, true, true, APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_KEEP },
+		/* A sleep that powers the SoC down: powered off, a PCIe tunnel refuses it */
+		{ true, false, true, false, APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_POWER_OFF },
+		{ true, true, true, false, APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_POWER_OFF },
+		{ true, false, true, true, APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_REFUSE },
+		{ true, true, true, true, APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_REFUSE },
+		/* Nothing connected, being removed or disabled for good: nothing to do */
+		{ true, true, false, true, APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_KEEP },
+		{ true, false, false, false, APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_KEEP },
+		{ false, true, false, false, APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_KEEP },
+	};
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(cases); i++)
+		KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ_MSG(test,
+				    apple_cio_sleep_action(cases[i].deep, cases[i].teardown,
+							   cases[i].connected, cases[i].pcie),
+				    cases[i].action, "case %u", i);
+}
+
+static struct kunit_case apple_cio_sleep_cases[] = {
+	KUNIT_CASE(apple_cio_sleep_action_test),
+	{},
+};
+
+static struct kunit_suite apple_cio_sleep_suite = {
+	.name = "apple-cio-sleep",
+	.test_cases = apple_cio_sleep_cases,
+};
+
+kunit_test_suite(apple_cio_sleep_suite);
diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c
index 7e6b10c828..482408678b 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/apple.c
@@ -110,6 +110,7 @@
 #include <linux/usb/typec_tbt.h>
 #include <linux/workqueue.h>
 
+#include "apple-cio-sleep.h"
 #include "apple-dpin-handshake.h"
 #include "apple-dpin-state.h"
 #include "apple-dpin-request.h"
@@ -268,6 +269,8 @@ static atomic_t resume_recovery_used = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
  * A connected port keeps its link trained through s2idle, which costs about
  * 1.3 W on t6000. Powering ACIO off for the sleep saves that, but drops every
  * tunnel: devices behind them are removed and rediscovered after resume.
+ * A sleep that powers the SoC down powers the ports off whatever this says,
+ * see apple_cio_sleep_action().
  */
 static bool s2idle_teardown;
 module_param(s2idle_teardown, bool, 0644);
@@ -3422,32 +3425,44 @@ static int apple_cio_tbt_switch_set(struct typec_thunderbolt_switch_dev *sw,
 	return 0;
 }
 
-static void apple_cio_sleep_teardown(struct apple_cio *acio)
+static int apple_cio_sleep_teardown(struct apple_cio *acio, bool deep)
 {
+	enum apple_cio_sleep_action action;
+	bool connected;
+
 	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&acio->resume_link_work);
 
 	guard(mutex)(&acio->lock);
-	if (acio->removing || acio->resume_recovery_failed || !acio->current_cable_info)
-		return;
+	connected = !acio->removing && !acio->resume_recovery_failed && acio->current_cable_info;
 
 	/*
 	 * PCIe endpoints may unregister PM notifiers on removal, which would
 	 * deadlock inside this notifier chain, and storage must not disappear
-	 * under running applications. Skip ports with PCIe tunnels, and close
-	 * PCIe admission in the same critical section; the next NHI probe
-	 * reopens it.
+	 * under running applications. Skip ports with PCIe tunnels, or refuse a
+	 * sleep that would take them down anyway, and close PCIe admission in
+	 * the same critical section; the next NHI probe reopens it.
 	 */
 	scoped_guard(mutex, &acio->pcie_tunnel_lock) {
-		if (READ_ONCE(acio->pcie_tunnel_requested) || acio->pcie_tunnel_populated)
-			return;
+		action = apple_cio_sleep_action(deep, READ_ONCE(s2idle_teardown), connected,
+						READ_ONCE(acio->pcie_tunnel_requested) ||
+						acio->pcie_tunnel_populated);
+		if (action == APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_REFUSE) {
+			dev_warn(acio->dev,
+				 "refusing deep sleep: a PCIe tunnel is up (cable info 0x%x), and the SoC power-down would take it and its devices\n",
+				 acio->current_cable_info);
+			return -EBUSY;
+		}
+		if (action != APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_POWER_OFF)
+			return 0;
 		acio->pcie_tunnel_stopping = true;
 	}
 
-	dev_info(acio->dev, "powering off port for system sleep (cable info 0x%x)\n",
-		 acio->current_cable_info);
+	dev_info(acio->dev, "powering off port for %s (cable info 0x%x)\n",
+		 deep ? "deep sleep" : "system sleep", acio->current_cable_info);
 	apple_cio_stop(acio);
 	acio->sleep_phase = APPLE_CIO_SLEEP_CLOSED;
 	WRITE_ONCE(acio->sleep_restart_owed, true);
+	return 0;
 }
 
 /* A restart still owed from the last sleep must not race this suspend. */
@@ -3489,10 +3504,12 @@ static int apple_cio_pm_notify(struct notifier_block *nb, unsigned long event,
 			       void *unused)
 {
 	struct apple_cio *acio = container_of(nb, struct apple_cio, pm_notifier);
+	bool deep = !pm_suspend_default_s2idle();
 	bool owed, early;
+	int ret;
 
-	/* Teardown off and no restart owed from an earlier sleep: nothing to do. */
-	if (!READ_ONCE(s2idle_teardown) && !READ_ONCE(acio->sleep_restart_owed))
+	/* No teardown for this sleep and no restart owed from an earlier one: nothing to do. */
+	if (!READ_ONCE(s2idle_teardown) && !deep && !READ_ONCE(acio->sleep_restart_owed))
 		return NOTIFY_DONE;
 
 	switch (event) {
@@ -3501,8 +3518,9 @@ static int apple_cio_pm_notify(struct notifier_block *nb, unsigned long event,
 		cancel_delayed_work_sync(&acio->sleep_restart_work);
 		apple_cio_sleep_close_owed(acio);
 		/* Before tasks freeze: tearing down the NHI needs unfrozen work. */
-		if (READ_ONCE(s2idle_teardown))
-			apple_cio_sleep_teardown(acio);
+		ret = apple_cio_sleep_teardown(acio, deep);
+		if (ret)
+			return notifier_from_errno(ret);
 		break;
 	case PM_POST_SUSPEND:
 		scoped_guard(mutex, &acio->lock) {
